      "title": "Pauline Hanson seems more and more like the de facto opposition leader. At CPAC, Angus Taylor warmed the seat for her",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/cpac-conference-pauline-hanson-angus-taylor-one-nation-liberals-cooperation-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Cooperation between One Nation and the Liberals ‘up to Angus Taylor’, Hanson says at conservative conference. How would that work?</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The annual Conservative Political Action Conference billed itself as a chance for the leaders of the three main conservative parties – Liberals, Nationals and One Nation – to make their pitch and compete for rightwing voters.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/10/angus-taylor-pauline-hanson-criticised-heckled-cpac-australia-brisbane-ntwnfb\">reception they received in Brisbane</a> on Saturday suggests Pauline Hanson is effectively the preferred opposition leader, with Angus Taylor relegated to leading a minor party.</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/aug/28/politics-really-newsletter-guardian-australia-free-best-daily-auspol-email-subscribe?CMP=copyembed\">Sign up for Guardian Australia’s Politics, really newsletter here</a></strong></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/cpac-conference-pauline-hanson-angus-taylor-one-nation-liberals-cooperation-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-10T19:00:09"
    },
    {
      "title": "Albanese wants universal childcare to be his legacy. But paying for it isn’t as easy as ABC",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/universal-childcare-cost-family-planning-politics",
      "description": "<p>Labor is running out of time to make its case to Australian families about how it is going to make childcare accessible and affordable for all</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">new political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Rachel Hill, a mother of two from Sydney, explains the maths of family planning.</p><p>The not-for-profit worker has bumped up her workload from two days a week to full-time to try to cover ever-rising costs. Instead, the additional money has been swallowed by childcare fees.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/universal-childcare-cost-family-planning-politics\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-10T19:00:07"
    },
    {
      "title": "Regional universities struggle to enrol international students amid visa refusals and heated political rhetoric",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/falling-international-student-numbers-australian-universities",
      "description": "<p>Expert says declining numbers are causing ‘massive problems’ as international students are ‘basically the only profitable aspect of university operations’</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>University leaders warn they’re struggling to fill international student places due to visa uncertainty and heated political debate on immigration, with regional areas hit the hardest amid concern that financial deficits could affect domestic students.</p><p>The latest numbers from the Department of Education in October show on average universities are filling just two-thirds of their allocated places, and just five public universities have reached more than 80% capacity, all in capital cities.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/oct/11/falling-international-student-numbers-australian-universities\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-10T19:00:05"
    },
    {
      "title": "Despair after 200-year-old ‘grandmother’ tree toppled for Trump’s border wall: ‘It crushes your heart’",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/10/cottonwood-tree-toppling-trump-mexico-border-wall-arizona",
      "description": "<p>Protesters spent months occupying an ancient cottonwood. Then a tense standoff with US border patrol came to a head</p><p>A 200-year-old cottonwood tree near the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-mexico-border\">US-Mexico border</a>, known as the “grandmother”, has been destroyed to make way for the construction of <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump\">Donald Trump</a>’s border wall, despite a months-long effort by protesters trying to save it.</p><p>In late July, land defenders began <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/04/protesters-occupy-tree-us-mexico-border\">a tree-sit</a> in the ghost town of Lochiel, <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/arizona\">Arizona</a>, taking turns occupying a platform high in the tree’s canopy. The group remained there for more than 70 days.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/10/cottonwood-tree-toppling-trump-mexico-border-wall-arizona\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-10T20:57:32"
    }
  ]
